// Expected: a1
//
// ENC is unauthenticated, so a stream-cipher-style ciphertext can be mauled:
// holding ENC(k, CONCAT(lo, lo)) the attacker builds a ciphertext under the
// same k carrying a payload of its own, without ever learning k. Bob's checked
// SPLIT then succeeds on a value Alice never sent, and he MACs a half of it.
//
// Bob reaches c only through the checked SPLIT, so when the attacker cannot
// maul, every substitution fails that check and halts him before c is used at
// all. That is what makes the counterweight sharp: cap_malleable_absent.vp is
// this same model without the annotation, and there the query holds. The pair
// keeps `malleable` from being either a no-op or a blanket authenticity break.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public lo
	c = ENC[malleable](k, CONCAT(lo, lo))
]

Alice -> Bob: c

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	x, y = SPLIT(DEC(k, c))?
	receipt = MAC(k, x)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: c
]
